Yes. With Spring Retry, the important thing is that the exception that triggers the retry is thrown from the method, and Spring Retry intercepts it outside your method. So you have a few good ways to identify it.

1. Simplest: add a @Recover method

A @Recover method is called after all retry attempts are exhausted, and it receives the exception that caused the retries.

@Recover
public void recover(DataIntegrityViolationException ex,
                    Set<Long> validZipCodes,
                    String customerId,
                    String facilityId,
                    String serviceLevel) {

    log.error(
        "processValidZipCodes failed after retries. customerId={}, facilityId={}, serviceLevel={}",
        customerId, facilityId, serviceLevel, ex
    );
}

This tells you the final exception that caused the retry sequence to eventually fail.

However, if you want to know the exception at the moment each retry starts, use a RetryListener.


---

2. Best option: RetryListener

Spring Retry provides RetryListener specifically for this.

You can create a listener:

@Component
@Slf4j
public class RetryLoggingListener implements RetryListener {

    @Override
    public <T, E extends Throwable> boolean open(
            RetryContext context,
            RetryCallback<T, E> callback) {

        return true;
    }

    @Override
    public <T, E extends Throwable> void onError(
            RetryContext context,
            RetryCallback<T, E> callback,
            Throwable throwable) {

        log.error(
            "Retry attempt {} failed for method {}. Exception: {} - {}",
            context.getRetryCount(),
            context.getAttribute(RetryContext.NAME),
            throwable.getClass().getName(),
            throwable.getMessage(),
            throwable
        );
    }

    @Override
    public <T, E extends Throwable> void close(
            RetryContext context,
            RetryCallback<T, E> callback,
            Throwable throwable) {

        if (throwable != null) {
            log.error(
                "Retry exhausted. Final exception: {} - {}",
                throwable.getClass().getName(),
                throwable.getMessage(),
                throwable
            );
        }
    }
}

Then associate it with your retry configuration.

For example:

@Retryable(
    retryFor = {DataIntegrityViolationException.class},
    maxAttempts = 3,
    backoff = @Backoff(delay = 100, maxDelay = 400, random = true),
    listeners = {"retryLoggingListener"}
)

Now you'll get something like:

Retry attempt 1 failed for method processValidZipCodes.
Exception: org.springframework.dao.DataIntegrityViolationException
Message: could not execute statement

Then:

Retry attempt 2 failed for method processValidZipCodes.
Exception: org.springframework.dao.DataIntegrityViolationException
Message: could not execute statement

And finally:

Retry exhausted. Final exception:
org.springframework.dao.DataIntegrityViolationException

3. If you only want to debug the actual DB exception

DataIntegrityViolationException is often just the outer exception. The actual reason may be buried in its cause.

For example:

DataIntegrityViolationException
    -> ConstraintViolationException
        -> SQLException
            -> ORA-00001: unique constraint violated

So log the complete exception:

log.error("Exception causing retry", throwable);

rather than only:

log.error("Exception causing retry: {}", throwable.getMessage());

The first one will print the full stack trace and cause chain.


