the DataIntegrityViolationException may actually be thrown at the flush() rather than at saveAll(). That's why your retry may appear to be happening "at the end" of the method.

For your specific requirement, I'd use RetryListener because it lets you see the exception and retry count for every retry attempt, rather than only after all 3 attempts fail.


Yes — and this is an important distinction.

Your current configuration:

@Retryable(
    retryFor = {DataIntegrityViolationException.class},
    maxAttempts = 3,
    backoff = @Backoff(delay = 100, maxDelay = 400, random = true)
)

already means only DataIntegrityViolationException is eligible for retry. A 404, 400 Bad Request, or a generic connection exception will not automatically be retried unless it is wrapped/translated into DataIntegrityViolationException.

If you want to explicitly control which exceptions are retryable

You can define the retryable exceptions:

@Retryable(
    retryFor = {
        DataIntegrityViolationException.class
    },
    maxAttempts = 3,
    backoff = @Backoff(
        delay = 100,
        maxDelay = 400,
        random = true
    )
)

Then your behavior is essentially:

Exception	Retry?

DataIntegrityViolationException	✅ Yes
BadRequestException / HTTP 400	❌ No
NotFoundException / HTTP 404	❌ No
Generic RuntimeException	❌ No
Generic connection exception	❌ No
NullPointerException	❌ No


However, if your DataIntegrityViolationException has different root causes, you may want more granular control.

If you want to inspect the actual cause before retrying

For example, you could have:

DataIntegrityViolationException
    └── ConstraintViolationException
          └── SQLException

The RetryListener can inspect the exception:

@Override
public <T, E extends Throwable> void onError(
        RetryContext context,
        RetryCallback<T, E> callback,
        Throwable throwable) {

    log.error(
        "Retry attempt {}. Exception: {}",
        context.getRetryCount(),
        throwable.getClass().getName(),
        throwable
    );
}

But if your requirement is:

> "Retry only certain exceptions and completely ignore 400, 404 and connectivity exceptions."



I'd recommend keeping the retry policy restrictive rather than catching everything and deciding inside the method.

For example:

@Retryable(
    retryFor = {
        DataIntegrityViolationException.class,
        OptimisticLockingFailureException.class
    },
    maxAttempts = 3,
    backoff = @Backoff(delay = 100, maxDelay = 400, random = true)
)

Only the exceptions that you explicitly put in retryFor will trigger retry.

One thing I'd clarify in your case

If by "basic connectivity exception" you mean something like:

ConnectException
SocketTimeoutException
SQLTransientConnectionException
DataAccessResourceFailureException

then whether you should retry those depends on where they occur.

For example, a temporary database connection failure is often reasonable to retry, while a permanent configuration/authentication failure is not.

So I wouldn't put a broad:

Exception.class

in retryFor.

Instead, define a small whitelist of genuinely transient exceptions.

