Got it. You want retrying to continue normally, but when an exception occurs, you want to capture/log only specific exceptions and ignore things like 400, 404, or generic connectivity exceptions for logging/handling.

In that case, don't change your @Retryable configuration. Let Spring Retry do its job, and filter the exception in the RetryListener.

For example:

@Override
public <T, E extends Throwable> void onError(
        RetryContext context,
        RetryCallback<T, E> callback,
        Throwable throwable) {

    if (throwable instanceof DataIntegrityViolationException) {
        log.error(
            "Retry attempt {} due to DataIntegrityViolationException",
            context.getRetryCount(),
            throwable
        );
    }
}

Your existing method stays:

