If you want multiple specific exceptions

You can prioritize them:

@Override
public <T, E extends Throwable> void onError(
        RetryContext context,
        RetryCallback<T, E> callback,
        Throwable throwable) {

    if (throwable instanceof DataIntegrityViolationException ex) {

        log.error(
            "Data integrity issue on retry attempt {}: {}",
            context.getRetryCount(),
            ex.getMessage(),
            ex
        );

    } else if (throwable instanceof OptimisticLockingFailureException ex) {

        log.error(
            "Optimistic locking issue on retry attempt {}: {}",
            context.getRetryCount(),
            ex.getMessage(),
            ex
        );

    }
    // Don't do anything for other exceptions
}

The important point is:

RetryListener.onError() does not decide whether the retry happens. It is just observing the failure.

So:

Exception occurs
      ↓
Spring Retry decides whether it is retryable
      ↓
onError() gets called
      ↓
You inspect/log the exception
      ↓
Spring continues with retry

Therefore, you can safely do:

if (throwable instanceof DataIntegrityViolationException) {
    // catch/log/inspect
}

without preventing the retry.

Also, if you're trying to identify the actual root cause inside DataIntegrityViolationException, that's slightly different. For example, you may want to distinguish unique constraint violation vs foreign-key violation vs some other DB issue. In that case, I can show you how to extract and prioritize the root SQLException from the exception chain.